  "findings": [
    "BAFU's published results are reproduced by its own openLCA database scored with the EF 3.1 package it ships: 99.68-99.77 % of processes within 1 % in every category. The 27-38 processes per category outside that are mostly results near zero; a handful (e.g. 'Disposal, non-sulfidic overburden', 'Treatment of fuel cell stack, 1MWe, SOEC') are not explained yet.",
    "Giving this route GreenDelta's number wherever both use the same flow (brightway-flows 1.1.0 crosswalk) leaves cancer toxicity, water use, ecotoxicity, marine eutrophication and non-cancer toxicity within half a percentage point of where they were. Two categories do move: resource use, fossils (97.5 -> 99.5 %) and ionising radiation (99.3 -> 99.7 %). The five differences come from which flow an exchange is assigned to, not from different numbers for the same flow.",
    "Cancer toxicity: the two implementations characterise unspecified chromium differently; this route uses EF's chromium flow, which carries Cr(VI)'s factors (the method difference decided in sentier-mappings round 7).",
    "Water use: BAFU applies country-specific water factors; this route installs global factors only. Separately, this route leaves the 'Chemically polluted water' and 'Waste water' returns unlinked.",
    "Marine eutrophication: 'Nitrogen' emitted to water is scored on this route as EF 'Nitrogen, total' (factor 1.0), a documented decision (sentier-mappings #15).",
    "Ionising radiation x1000 in 20 processes is an inventory unit reading: BAFU's ecoSpold states Carbon-14 and Radon-222 in Bq where its openLCA database holds the same numbers in kBq (F12)."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Shows which factor set and flow assignment reproduce BAFU's numbers, not which is correct.",
    "'Same flow' means the brightway-flows 1.1.0 consensus crosswalk; 855 of the 2,460 openLCA flows used have no consensus id, so on 5,778 of 21,720 matrix rows a number difference and an identity difference cannot be told apart.",
    "openLCA's calculation was reconstructed, not run; the 99.7 % agreement supports the reconstruction."
  ],
  "measured_by": "Manuel Klarmann (Eaternity), agent-run measurement"
}
